  { q: 'What do the ravens symbolise in Völuspá?', a: 'In Völuspá (the most famous eddic poem, the "Prophecy of the Seeress"), ravens appear in the context of Odin and the mythological world order. The poem describes the ravens as part of Odin\'s identity — symbols of his knowledge-seeking nature. At Ragnarök, the destruction of the cosmic order is foretold, and Odin\'s ravens are implicitly part of the world that will end. The seeress\'s vision encompasses the ravens along with Yggdrasil, the world tree, and all the other fixtures of the Norse cosmos.' },
